**Why it looks mechanical:** right now Title moves from 0.5 s to 1.5 s at a constant speed. It starts at full speed and stops dead. Easing makes it speed up out of the first keyframe and slow down into the next, which is how real objects move.

**What I just did:** I eased **Title › Position between 0.5 s and 1.5 s**. **Edit > Undo** reverses it if you want to see the before and after.

**Where to look:**
- **In the timeline:** select Title and press **U** to show its keyframes. The keys at 0.5 s and 1.5 s are no longer diamonds; they're now hourglass-shaped.
- **In the Graph Editor:** turn on the Graph Editor (the button above the layers), click Position, and right-click the graph to choose **Edit Speed Graph**. The speed for that stretch is now a smooth hill instead of a flat line.
- **In playback:** watch 0.5–1.5 s. Title glides out and settles in.

**Your turn: three pairs on Title.**
- **Position, 1.5 s → 3 s.** The key at 1.5 s may look done already, but the demo only eased the side facing the first pair. Select both keys here.
- **Scale, 0.5 s → 1.5 s**
- **Opacity, 0.5 s → 1.5 s**

For each one, select both keys, then go to **Animation > Keyframe Assistant > Easy Ease** (or press **F9**). Say "done" when you're ready and I'll check.

